{
 "id": "2026-nole-bathroom",
 "name": "Bathroom Cladding Materials and Perceived Well-Being (5 Renderings)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the online experiment in Nolé, Pagan, Montañana & Llinares (2026), \"Influence of Bathroom Cladding Materials on Users' Perceived Well-Being\", Architecture 6(2):52. Participants see the paper's five renderings of the same bathroom (SketchUp + V-Ray, same layout, skylight and fixtures) that differ only in cladding on the floor, shower walls and vanity top: ceramic, wood, vinyl, natural stone and microcement, each shown on its own page with its material label. For each bathroom they rate \"Overall, this bathroom: generates well-being / generates stress / is functional / I like it / is expensive or high-priced / I would choose it for my home\" on a 7-point agreement scale coded −3 (strongly disagree) to +3 (strongly agree), and emotional valence on a 9-point scale. Sociodemographic questions follow (age, gender, country of birth, place of residence, household size, dependents, construction-sector profession, bathroom size). In the paper (N = 58, Friedman tests), wood and natural stone gave more well-being and more pleasant emotions, and microcement was liked least, felt most stressful and was seen as cheapest. Renderings are cropped from the paper's Figure 2 (CC BY 4.0); credit to the authors, and the template will be removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the five bathrooms appear in random order (the paper does not state the order); (2) valence uses a 1–9 scale with text anchors (very unpleasant to very pleasant) instead of the Self-Assessment Manikin figures (the paper describes it as 0–9); (3) the renderings come from the published figure (about 760 × 850 px) rather than the original 1600 × 1800 px files, with the material name shown as text under each image; (4) the sociodemographic questions are rebuilt from the sample description, so their wording is ours.",
 "author": "Nolé, M. L., Pagan, A., Montañana, A., Llinares, C.",
